WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.680
Part 3. You will hear two marine biology students discussing a lecture on marine

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.760
biodiversity and conservation from a visiting speaker.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.559
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:54.240
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:01.919
That lecture from the visiting speaker yesterday was good, wasn't it?

00:01:01.919 --> 00:01:08.639
Yeah. I learned a lot from her about ocean biodiversity. I've already done some reading

00:01:08.639 --> 00:01:13.919
on it and I did an assignment on some of the problems associated with it last year.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.959
But I especially liked the way her lecture focused on more long-term issues.

00:01:19.519 --> 00:01:24.000
Yes, things that aren't currently receiving widespread attention

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.160
but are likely to be important in the future. That impressed me too. It wasn't exactly a

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.839
feel-good conclusion because it's hard to see any real solution for a lot of the problems.

00:01:35.839 --> 00:01:39.440
No, though she did point to where policy changes

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.800
could be made to protect our marine and coastal environments.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:50.160
But that's just at a national level. The examples she gave were at a more global level

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.080
and they really made it clear to me just how wide-ranging the threats to ocean biodiversity are.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.040
Yes, me too.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.480
The research project she described was impressive, wasn't it? I'd have thought

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.559
it was quite unusual to have so many experts working together.

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:12.080
Yeah, and from such different backgrounds. Must have been a really exciting team to work with.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:19.039
I'd heard of a couple of them before. They were involved in research way back in 2009,

00:02:19.039 --> 00:02:22.000
warning about the dangers of ocean pollution.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.199
But now people are much more aware of that, aren't they?

00:02:25.199 --> 00:02:26.479
I suppose so.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:31.600
Another thing about the research is that the team members came from all round the world.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.360
Though I suppose that's not unusual nowadays. Now everyone can work remotely.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:43.679
Right. I like the way she didn't bombard us with figures. I mean, they were available

00:02:43.679 --> 00:02:47.440
but she focused more on the general points they indicated.

00:02:48.479 --> 00:02:54.000
And the description of improvements in systems used for tracking marine animals

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.279
and things like robots were really interesting.

00:02:57.279 --> 00:03:04.160
Yes, and her description of how robotics can be used to investigate threats to biodiversity.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:04.880
Absolutely.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:16.320
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:55.839
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.880
While you're here, can we talk about the list of resources

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.279
we have to evaluate for the seminar tomorrow?

00:04:05.919 --> 00:04:09.679
I've had a look at them all but it's been a bit of a rush.

00:04:09.679 --> 00:04:14.160
Yeah. What did you think of that article on invasive lionfish?

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.679
The one claiming they were expanding their habitat throughout the Mediterranean Sea?

00:04:19.679 --> 00:04:25.760
Well, the writer went on about how dangerous they were in environmental terms,

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.600
which is probably true, but he didn't really provide much information to explain why.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.480
I know what you mean.

00:04:33.119 --> 00:04:37.679
I watched the documentary on microplastics, at least I started to,

00:04:38.239 --> 00:04:41.839
but then I found it was made ten years ago so I gave up.

00:04:42.399 --> 00:04:46.160
I watched to the end but you're right, it was showing its age.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.839
People had hardly heard of microplastics then,

00:04:49.839 --> 00:04:53.760
whereas now everyone knows about them and how dangerous they are.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.679
Yeah. Did you listen to the podcast on ocean pollution?

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.880
Hmm. I didn't get anything out of it though. Most of it was stating the obvious.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.760
Yes. It mentioned pesticides and plastic and things

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.279
and it clearly made the point that they were a bad thing.

00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:17.119
But everybody knows that anyway. Did you read that book on coastal ecosystems?

00:05:17.119 --> 00:05:18.480
The one by John Harper?

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.320
Yes. I found it hard going at first.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:28.720
It went into a lot of detail about things like the effects of offshore wind farms and fish farms

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.119
but actually I ended up with a much better understanding of the issues.

00:05:33.119 --> 00:05:37.440
Yes, I agree and I thought it was a well-written summary of those

00:05:38.079 --> 00:05:39.920
and the diagrams helped a lot too.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.160
The article on metal toxicity was way above my head.

00:05:44.799 --> 00:05:49.839
I didn't know anything about how metals from industrial emissions react in the ocean

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.559
and I still don't understand it.

00:05:53.359 --> 00:05:57.279
I gave up reading after the first chapter. I just couldn't follow it.

00:05:58.079 --> 00:06:01.600
That podcast on floating marine cities was interesting,

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.079
though it presented a rather one-sided picture, I thought.

00:06:06.079 --> 00:06:09.760
Yes. It focused on how this would benefit people

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.000
and ignored the effects on the environment.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:14.640
But anyway, shall we talk about…

00:06:15.119 --> 00:06:17.119
That is the end of part three.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.440
You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part three.

